# More people of the Mabinogion (November 2026): the people of Culhwch and Olwen, Macsen, Lludd and Llefelys,
# the Dream of Rhonabwy, the romances and the Tale of Taliesin, and Manawydan's enemy Llwyd. mabinogi_people.py
# adds them to its list. Same format and house rules as mabinogi_people.py: told in the words of the tales, with respect.
from people2a import P

ART = "Age of Arthur"

PEOPLE = [
 P("llwyd-fab-cil-coed", "ancient", "The Four Branches", "Llwyd son of Cil Coed", "Llwyd fab Cil Coed", "The enchanter of the Third Branch", "Dewin y Drydedd Gainc", "Llwyd_ap_Cil_Coed", "heroes",
   "The enchanter who cast the spell over Dyfed in the Third Branch, to avenge his friend Gwawl, and who lifted it when Manawydan caught his wife as a mouse.",
   "Y dewin a fwriodd yr hud dros Ddyfed yn y Drydedd Gainc, i ddial cam ei gyfaill Gwawl, ac a’i cododd pan ddaliodd Manawydan ei wraig ar ffurf llygoden.",
   ["Llwyd son of Cil Coed is the hidden hand behind the Third Branch of the Mabinogi. Years before, in the First Branch, Pwyll had tricked Gwawl son of Clud into a bag at the wedding feast and won Rhiannon from him. Llwyd was Gwawl’s friend, and he never forgot it.",
    "He laid an enchantment on the seven cantrefs of Dyfed, so that every house and every person and animal vanished, leaving only Manawydan, Rhiannon, Pryderi and Cigfa. He led Pryderi and Rhiannon into a magic fort and held them there with a golden bowl, and he sent his war-band as mice to eat Manawydan’s wheat.",
    "Manawydan caught one mouse, heavier than the rest, and set up a gallows on Gorsedd Arberth to hang it. One after another a clerk, a priest and a bishop came by and offered rich gifts to save it. It was Llwyd himself in disguise, and the mouse was his wife, who was expecting a child. Manawydan made him lift the enchantment from Dyfed, bring back Pryderi and Rhiannon, and swear never to take revenge again.",
    "Llwyd is one of the great enchanters of Welsh tradition, and his story shows the old law of the tales: a wrong done is never forgotten, and the wise man wins not by strength but by patience."],
   ["Llwyd fab Cil Coed yw’r llaw gudd y tu ôl i Drydedd Gainc y Mabinogi. Flynyddoedd ynghynt, yn y Gainc Gyntaf, twyllasai Pwyll Wawl fab Clud i god yn y wledd briodas ac ennill Rhiannon oddi arno. Cyfaill i Wawl oedd Llwyd, ac nid anghofiodd hynny byth.",
    "Bwriodd hud ar saith cantref Dyfed, fel y diflannodd pob tŷ a phob dyn ac anifail, gan adael dim ond Manawydan, Rhiannon, Pryderi a Chigfa. Arweiniodd Bryderi a Rhiannon i gaer hud a’u dal yno â ffiol aur, ac anfonodd ei osgordd ar ffurf llygod i fwyta gwenith Manawydan.",
    "Daliodd Manawydan un llygoden, trymach na’r lleill, a chodi crocbren ar Orsedd Arberth i’w chrogi. Fesul un daeth ysgolhaig, offeiriad ac esgob heibio a chynnig rhoddion cyfoethog i’w hachub. Llwyd ei hun oedd ef dan rith, a’r llygoden oedd ei wraig, a oedd yn feichiog. Gwnaeth Manawydan iddo godi’r hud oddi ar Ddyfed, dod â Phryderi a Rhiannon yn ôl, a thyngu na ddialai byth eto.",
    "Mae Llwyd yn un o ddewiniaid mawr traddodiad Cymru, ac mae ei stori’n dangos hen gyfraith y chwedlau: nid anghofir cam byth, ac nid â nerth y mae’r doeth yn ennill ond ag amynedd."]),
 P("ysbaddaden", "saints", ART, "Ysbaddaden Bencawr", "Ysbaddaden Bencawr", "Chief of giants, father of Olwen", "Pencawr, tad Olwen", "Ysbaddaden", "heroes",
   "The chief of giants in Culhwch and Olwen, father of Olwen, who set Culhwch nearly forty impossible tasks before he would give up his daughter.",
   "Pencawr y cewri yn Culhwch ac Olwen, tad Olwen, a osododd bron i ddeugain o anoethau i Gulhwch cyn y rhoddai ei ferch.",
   ["Ysbaddaden Bencawr, Ysbaddaden chief of giants, is the great obstacle of Culhwch and Olwen, the oldest Arthurian tale. His fort lay beyond a wide plain, guarded by nine porters and nine watchdogs, and his eyelids were so heavy that his servants had to lift them with forked props before he could see.",
    "It was said that he would live only until his daughter married, so he did everything he could to stop it. Three times he threw a poisoned stone spear at Culhwch and Arthur’s men, and three times they threw it back at him. Then he set his tasks, the anoethau, each one ‘hard to get’, from ploughing a great hill in a day to the comb and shears between the ears of the boar Twrch Trwyth.",
    "When every task was done, Caw of Prydyn shaved him, and Ysbaddaden gave Olwen to Culhwch, saying that he owed thanks to Arthur and not to the giant. Then Goreu son of Custennin, whose brothers the giant had killed, cut off his head.",
    "Ysbaddaden belongs to a very old kind of tale, the giant’s daughter who is won by impossible tasks, and the Welsh telling is one of the finest in the world. His list of tasks is a treasury of early Welsh tradition, naming heroes, hounds, cauldrons and wonders found nowhere else."],
   ["Ysbaddaden Bencawr yw rhwystr mawr Culhwch ac Olwen, y chwedl Arthuraidd hynaf. Safai ei gaer y tu hwnt i wastad llydan, dan ofal naw porthor a naw gwyliwr, ac roedd ei amrannau mor drwm fel y rhaid oedd i’w weision eu codi â ffyrch cyn y gallai weld.",
    "Dywedid na fyddai byw ond nes i’w ferch briodi, felly gwnaeth bopeth a allai i rwystro hynny. Deirgwaith taflodd waywffon garreg wenwynig at Gulhwch a gwŷr Arthur, a theirgwaith y taflasant hi’n ôl ato. Yna gosododd ei anoethau, bob un yn ‘anodd ei gael’, o aredig bryn mawr mewn diwrnod i’r grib a’r gwellaif rhwng dwy glust y Twrch Trwyth.",
    "Wedi gorffen pob anoeth, eilliodd Caw o Brydyn ef, a rhoddodd Ysbaddaden Olwen i Gulhwch, gan ddweud mai i Arthur yr oedd y diolch ac nid i’r cawr. Yna torrodd Goreu fab Custennin, y lladdasai’r cawr ei frodyr, ei ben.",
    "Perthyn Ysbaddaden i fath hen iawn o chwedl, merch y cawr a enillir drwy anoethau, ac mae’r fersiwn Cymraeg yn un o’r goreuon yn y byd. Mae ei restr o anoethau’n drysorfa o hen draddodiad Cymru, yn enwi arwyr, cŵn, peiri a rhyfeddodau na cheir mohonynt yn unman arall."]),
 P("beli-mawr", "ancient", "1st century BC", "Beli Mawr", "Beli Mawr", "Great king of Britain, forefather of kings", "Brenin mawr Prydain, cyndad brenhinoedd", "Beli_Mawr", "rulers",
   "Beli the Great, son of Manogan, king of the Island of Britain in Welsh tradition, father of Caswallon, Lludd and Llefelys, and the forefather named at the head of the pedigrees of the kings of Wales.",
   "Beli Mawr fab Manogan, brenin Ynys Prydain yn nhraddodiad Cymru, tad Caswallon, Lludd a Llefelys, a’r cyndad a enwir ar ben achau brenhinoedd Cymru.",
   ["Beli Mawr son of Manogan stands at the head of Welsh tradition about the kings of the Island of Britain before the Romans came. In the tale of Lludd and Llefelys he is the father of four sons, Caswallon, Lludd, Nynnio and Llefelys, and when he died the kingdom passed to Lludd.",
    "His name is everywhere in the old pedigrees. The kings of Gwynedd traced their line back through Cunedda to Beli, and the genealogies of other Welsh kingdoms, and of the Men of the North, do the same. In the Four Branches, Caswallon son of Beli takes the crown of London while Bendigeidfran is in Ireland, and in the Dream of Macsen Wledig it is from ‘Beli son of Manogan and his sons’ that Macsen wins the Island.",
    "Many scholars link his name with Belinos, a god of the Britons, and his son Caswallon with Cassivellaunus, the British king who fought Julius Caesar in 54 BC. For the poets and genealogists of Wales, Beli Mawr was simply the great forefather: to be descended from Beli was to be of the old royal blood of the Island of Britain."],
   ["Saif Beli Mawr fab Manogan ar ben traddodiad Cymru am frenhinoedd Ynys Prydain cyn i’r Rhufeiniaid ddod. Yn chwedl Lludd a Llefelys mae’n dad i bedwar mab, Caswallon, Lludd, Nynnio a Llefelys, a phan fu farw aeth y deyrnas i Ludd.",
    "Mae ei enw ym mhobman yn yr hen achau. Olrheiniai brenhinoedd Gwynedd eu llinach yn ôl drwy Gunedda at Feli, ac felly achau teyrnasoedd eraill Cymru, a Gwŷr y Gogledd. Yn y Pedair Cainc, Caswallon fab Beli sy’n cipio coron Llundain tra bo Bendigeidfran yn Iwerddon, ac ym Mreuddwyd Macsen Wledig oddi ar ‘Feli fab Manogan a’i feibion’ y mae Macsen yn ennill yr Ynys.",
    "Mae llawer o ysgolheigion yn cysylltu ei enw â Belinos, un o dduwiau’r Brythoniaid, a’i fab Caswallon â Cassivellaunus, y brenin Brythonig a ymladdodd yn erbyn Iŵl Cesar yn 54 CC. I feirdd ac achyddwyr Cymru, Beli Mawr oedd y cyndad mawr: bod o hil Beli oedd bod o hen waed brenhinol Ynys Prydain."]),
 P("lludd", "ancient", "1st century BC", "Lludd son of Beli", "Lludd fab Beli", "King of Britain who rebuilt London", "Brenin Prydain a ailgododd Lundain", "Lludd_Llaw_Eraint", "rulers",
   "King of the Island of Britain in Welsh tradition, who rebuilt London, Caer Lludd, and with the help of his brother Llefelys freed the Island from three plagues and buried the two dragons at Dinas Emrys.",
   "Brenin Ynys Prydain yn nhraddodiad Cymru, a ailgododd Lundain, Caer Ludd, ac a ryddhaodd yr Ynys o dair gormes gyda chymorth ei frawd Llefelys, a chladdu’r ddwy ddraig yn Ninas Emrys.",
   ["Lludd son of Beli Mawr became king of the Island of Britain when his father died, and Welsh tradition remembers him as a good and generous king. He rebuilt the walls of London with many towers, and the city was called Caer Lludd after him, and later Caer Lundain. The Welsh Brut says that he was buried beside the gate still called by his name, Ludgate.",
    "In the tale of Lludd and Llefelys, three plagues fell on his kingdom: the Coraniaid, who heard every word the wind touched; a terrible scream every May Eve; and a giant who carried off the food of his court. Lludd sailed to France to ask the advice of his brother Llefelys, and they talked through a long horn of bronze so that the Coraniaid could not hear them.",
    "Following his brother’s counsel, Lludd destroyed the Coraniaid with water full of crushed insects, caught the two fighting dragons with a vat of mead at Oxford, the centre of the Island, and buried them in a stone chest at Dinas Emrys in Eryri. Then he stayed awake through the night, plunging into cold water whenever sleep came, and overcame the giant. After that he ruled Britain in peace.",
    "Lludd’s story is the beginning of the tale of the red dragon, which the boy Emrys found again under Dinas Emrys in the time of Gwrtheyrn. Some scholars hear in his name the old god Nudd, Nodens, who had a great temple at Lydney on the Severn."],
   ["Daeth Lludd fab Beli Mawr yn frenin Ynys Prydain pan fu farw ei dad, ac mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei gofio fel brenin da a hael. Ailgododd furiau Llundain â llawer o dyrau, a galwyd y ddinas yn Gaer Ludd ar ei ôl, ac wedyn yn Gaer Lundain. Dywed y Brut Cymraeg iddo gael ei gladdu wrth y porth a elwir ar ei enw o hyd, Porth Lludd.",
    "Yn chwedl Lludd a Llefelys, syrthiodd tair gormes ar ei deyrnas: y Coraniaid, a glywai bob gair a gyffyrddai’r gwynt; sgrech ofnadwy bob Nos Galan Mai; a chawr a ddygai fwyd ei lys. Hwyliodd Lludd i Ffrainc i ofyn cyngor ei frawd Llefelys, a siaradasant drwy gorn hir o efydd fel na allai’r Coraniaid eu clywed.",
    "Gan ddilyn cyngor ei frawd, difethodd Lludd y Coraniaid â dŵr yn llawn pryfed wedi’u malu, dal y ddwy ddraig â cherwyn o fedd yn Rhydychen, canol yr Ynys, a’u claddu mewn cist faen yn Ninas Emrys yn Eryri. Yna arhosodd yn effro drwy’r nos, gan fynd i ddŵr oer pryd bynnag y deuai cwsg, a gorchfygu’r cawr. Wedi hynny teyrnasodd ar Brydain mewn heddwch.",
    "Stori Lludd yw dechrau hanes y ddraig goch, a gafodd y bachgen Emrys eto dan Ddinas Emrys yn amser Gwrtheyrn. Mae rhai ysgolheigion yn clywed yn ei enw yr hen dduw Nudd, Nodens, a oedd â theml fawr yn Lydney ar Hafren."]),
 P("llefelys", "ancient", "1st century BC", "Llefelys son of Beli", "Llefelys fab Beli", "King of France, the wise brother", "Brenin Ffrainc, y brawd doeth", "Lludd_and_Llefelys", "rulers",
   "Son of Beli Mawr, who won the kingdom of France by marrying its heiress, and whose wise counsel freed the Island of Britain from three plagues in the tale of Lludd and Llefelys.",
   "Mab Beli Mawr, a enillodd deyrnas Ffrainc drwy briodi ei haeres, ac y rhyddhaodd ei gyngor doeth Ynys Prydain o dair gormes yn chwedl Lludd a Llefelys.",
   ["Llefelys was the youngest son of Beli Mawr, and his brother Lludd loved him more than all his other brothers, for he was wise and prudent. When the king of France died leaving only a daughter, Llefelys sailed with a fleet that Lludd gave him, married the heiress with the consent of the nobles of France, and ruled that kingdom well all his life.",
    "When the three plagues fell on Britain, Lludd came to France to ask his advice. Knowing that the Coraniaid heard every word the wind touched, Llefelys had a long bronze horn made so that the brothers could speak privately. When a demon in the horn turned their words into hateful ones, he had it washed out with wine.",
    "Then he told Lludd how to deal with each plague: the insects to crush in water against the Coraniaid, the vat of mead at the centre of the Island to catch the dragons, and the vat of cold water to keep awake and catch the giant. Every word of his counsel came true.",
    "Llefelys is the model of the wise counsellor in Welsh tradition, and the tale of the two brothers is a story of brotherly love and good counsel as much as of wonders."],
   ["Llefelys oedd mab ieuengaf Beli Mawr, a châi ei frawd Lludd ef yn anwylach na’i holl frodyr eraill, gan ei fod yn ddoeth a phwyllog. Pan fu farw brenin Ffrainc heb adael ond merch, hwyliodd Llefelys gyda llynges a roddodd Lludd iddo, priodi’r aeres gyda chaniatâd uchelwyr Ffrainc, a llywodraethu’r deyrnas honno’n dda weddill ei oes.",
    "Pan syrthiodd y tair gormes ar Brydain, daeth Lludd i Ffrainc i ofyn ei gyngor. Gan wybod y clywai’r Coraniaid bob gair a gyffyrddai’r gwynt, gwnaeth Llefelys gorn hir o efydd fel y gallai’r brodyr siarad yn breifat. Pan drodd cythraul yn y corn eu geiriau’n rhai atgas, golchodd ef â gwin.",
    "Yna dywedodd wrth Ludd sut i drechu pob gormes: y pryfed i’w malu mewn dŵr yn erbyn y Coraniaid, y gerwyn fedd yng nghanol yr Ynys i ddal y dreigiau, a’r gerwyn o ddŵr oer i aros yn effro a dal y cawr. Daeth pob gair o’i gyngor yn wir.",
    "Llefelys yw patrwm y cynghorwr doeth yn nhraddodiad Cymru, ac mae chwedl y ddau frawd yn stori am gariad brawdol a chyngor da gymaint ag am ryfeddodau."]),
 P("cynan-meiriadog", "roman", "4th century", "Cynan Meiriadog", "Cynan Meiriadog", "Founder of Brittany in Welsh and Breton tradition", "Sylfaenydd Llydaw yn nhraddodiad Cymru a Llydaw", "Conan_Meriadoc", "rulers",
   "Brother of Elen Luyddog, who in the Dream of Macsen Wledig helped win Rome for Macsen and then made his home in Llydaw, Brittany, with the men of Britain.",
   "Brawd Elen Luyddog, a helpodd ym Mreuddwyd Macsen Wledig i ennill Rhufain i Facsen ac yna wneud ei gartref yn Llydaw gyda gwŷr Prydain.",
   ["Cynan son of Eudaf, called Cynan Meiriadog, was the brother of Elen Luyddog, the British wife of the emperor Macsen Wledig. In the Dream of Macsen Wledig he and his brother Gadeon came from Britain with a small host when Macsen had besieged Rome for a year in vain. While the two emperors were at their midday meal, the men of Britain set ladders against the walls and took the city for Macsen.",
    "As a reward Macsen gave them his host to win lands of their own. They fought for years across Gaul, and at last Cynan chose to stay in the land they had won, while Gadeon went home. That land became Llydaw, Brittany, and its people kept the language of the Island of Britain.",
    "Breton tradition remembers him as Conan Meriadec, the first king of Brittany, and the old Breton dukes traced their line back to him. Geoffrey of Monmouth and the Welsh Brut tell his story too. Whatever the truth of it, the Bretons did come from Britain in the fourth to sixth centuries, and their language is the sister of Welsh to this day."],
   ["Brawd Elen Luyddog, gwraig Frythonig yr ymerawdwr Macsen Wledig, oedd Cynan fab Eudaf, a elwid Cynan Meiriadog. Ym Mreuddwyd Macsen Wledig daeth ef a’i frawd Gadeon o Brydain gyda llu bychan pan fuasai Macsen yn gwarchae ar Rufain am flwyddyn yn ofer. Tra oedd y ddau ymerawdwr wrth eu bwyd ganol dydd, gosododd gwŷr Prydain ysgolion wrth y muriau a chymryd y ddinas i Facsen.",
    "Yn wobr rhoddodd Macsen ei lu iddynt i ennill tiroedd iddynt eu hunain. Buont yn ymladd am flynyddoedd ar draws Gâl, ac o’r diwedd dewisodd Cynan aros yn y wlad a enillasant, tra aeth Gadeon adref. Daeth y wlad honno’n Llydaw, a chadwodd ei phobl iaith Ynys Prydain.",
    "Mae traddodiad Llydaw yn ei gofio fel Conan Meriadec, brenin cyntaf Llydaw, ac olrheiniai hen ddugiaid Llydaw eu llinach yn ôl ato. Mae Sieffre o Fynwy a’r Brut Cymraeg yn adrodd ei hanes hefyd. Beth bynnag am hynny, daeth y Llydawiaid o Brydain rhwng y bedwaredd a’r chweched ganrif, ac mae eu hiaith yn chwaer i’r Gymraeg hyd heddiw."]),
 P("rhonabwy", "medieval", "12th century", "Rhonabwy", "Rhonabwy", "The dreamer of Powys", "Breuddwydiwr Powys", "The_Dream_of_Rhonabwy", "words",
   "A man of Powys in the time of Madog ap Maredudd who, in the Dream of Rhonabwy, slept for three nights on a yellow ox-skin and saw the court of the Emperor Arthur on the eve of the battle of Badon.",
   "Gŵr o Bowys yn amser Madog ap Maredudd a gysgodd, ym Mreuddwyd Rhonabwy, dair noson ar groen dyniawed melyn a gweld llys yr Ymerawdwr Arthur ar drothwy brwydr Baddon.",
   ["Rhonabwy was one of the men sent by Madog ap Maredudd, king of Powys, to search for Madog’s brother Iorwerth, who had gone raiding into England. With two companions he took shelter for the night in the smoky, filthy hall of Heilyn Goch in Didlystwn, near Welshpool, and lay down on a yellow ox-skin to sleep.",
    "He dreamed that he was riding across the plain of Argyngroeg towards Rhyd-y-groes on the Severn, where he found the Emperor Arthur seated on a little island in the river. Iddog Cordd Prydain was his guide. He saw Arthur’s warbands ride past in all their colours, watched Arthur and Owain son of Urien play gwyddbwyll while Owain’s ravens fought Arthur’s young men, and saw the truce made with Osla Gyllellfawr before the battle of Badon.",
    "He woke after three nights and three days. His Dream, kept only in the Red Book of Hergest, is the most colourful and clever of all the Mabinogion tales, and it ends by saying that no bard or storyteller can tell it without a book."],
   ["Roedd Rhonabwy yn un o’r gwŷr a anfonodd Madog ap Maredudd, brenin Powys, i chwilio am Iorwerth, brawd Madog, a aethai i anrheithio yn Lloegr. Gyda dau gydymaith cafodd gysgod am y nos yn neuadd fyglyd, fudr Heilyn Goch yn Nidlystwn, ger y Trallwng, a gorwedd ar groen dyniawed melyn i gysgu.",
    "Breuddwydiodd ei fod yn marchogaeth ar draws maes Argyngroeg tua Rhyd-y-groes ar Hafren, lle cafodd yr Ymerawdwr Arthur yn eistedd ar ynys fechan yn yr afon. Iddog Cordd Prydain oedd ei dywysydd. Gwelodd osgorddion Arthur yn marchogaeth heibio yn eu holl liwiau, gwylio Arthur ac Owain fab Urien yn chwarae gwyddbwyll tra ymladdai brain Owain â gweision ifanc Arthur, a gweld y cadoediad a wnaed ag Osla Gyllellfawr cyn brwydr Baddon.",
    "Deffrôdd wedi tair noson a thridiau. Ei Freuddwyd, a gedwir yn Llyfr Coch Hergest yn unig, yw’r fwyaf lliwgar a chlyfar o holl chwedlau’r Mabinogion, ac mae’n gorffen drwy ddweud na all na bardd na chyfarwydd ei hadrodd heb lyfr."]),
 P("luned", "saints", ART, "Luned", "Luned", "The clever maiden of the Lady of the Fountain", "Morwyn glyfar Iarlles y Ffynnon", "Lunete", "heroes",
   "The maiden of the Lady of the Fountain in the romance of Owain, who saved Owain with a ring of invisibility, won him her mistress’s hand, and was saved by him in turn.",
   "Morwyn Iarlles y Ffynnon yn rhamant Owain, a achubodd Owain â modrwy anweledig, ennill llaw ei meistres iddo, ac a achubwyd ganddo yntau yn ei thro.",
   ["Luned is the real heroine of Owain, or the Lady of the Fountain. When Owain was trapped between the two gates of the castle of the fountain, it was Luned who came to him, because he had once been kind to her at Arthur’s court when nobody else would speak to her. She gave him a ring: ‘Put the stone inside your hand and close your fist over it, and as long as you hide it, it will hide you.’",
    "She hid him and cared for him, and then, with clever words, persuaded her grieving mistress that the fountain needed a defender and that Owain was the best man in the world. So Owain married the Lady of the Fountain.",
    "Later, when Owain had forgotten his wife for three years, Luned was shut in a stone vault and condemned to be burned for speaking up for him. Owain came back in time, fought her two accusers with his lion beside him, and set her free. Luned shows the qualities the tales praise most: loyalty, courage and quick wits."],
   ["Luned yw gwir arwres Owain, neu Iarlles y Ffynnon. Pan ddaliwyd Owain rhwng dau borth caer y ffynnon, Luned a ddaeth ato, am iddo fod yn garedig wrthi unwaith yn llys Arthur pan na siaradai neb arall â hi. Rhoddodd fodrwy iddo: ‘Dod y maen o fewn dy law a chau dy ddwrn amdano, a thra cuddi di ef, fe’th guddia di.’",
    "Cuddiodd ef a gofalu amdano, ac yna, â geiriau clyfar, perswadio ei meistres alarus fod ar y ffynnon angen amddiffynnydd ac mai Owain oedd y gŵr gorau yn y byd. Felly priododd Owain Iarlles y Ffynnon.",
    "Yn ddiweddarach, pan anghofiasai Owain ei wraig am dair blynedd, caewyd Luned mewn cell garreg a’i chondemnio i’w llosgi am siarad drosto. Daeth Owain yn ôl mewn pryd, ymladd â’i dau gyhuddwr â’i lew wrth ei ochr, a’i rhyddhau. Mae Luned yn dangos y rhinweddau a glodforir fwyaf yn y chwedlau: ffyddlondeb, dewrder a meddwl chwim."]),
 P("cynon-ap-clydno", "saints", "fl. 6th century", "Cynon son of Clydno", "Cynon fab Clydno", "Warrior of the Gododdin and teller of the tale of the fountain", "Rhyfelwr y Gododdin ac adroddwr chwedl y ffynnon", "Cynon_ap_Clydno", "heroes",
   "A warrior of the Old North praised in Y Gododdin, and in the romance of Owain the knight of Arthur’s court whose tale of the fountain sent Owain on his adventure.",
   "Rhyfelwr o’r Hen Ogledd a glodforir yn Y Gododdin, ac yn rhamant Owain y marchog o lys Arthur a anfonodd ei chwedl am y ffynnon Owain ar ei antur.",
   ["Cynon son of Clydno Eidyn was a warrior of the Old North, the British kingdoms of what is now southern Scotland and northern England. Aneirin’s great poem Y Gododdin praises him among the war-band of Mynyddog Mwynfawr who rode from Din Eidyn, Edinburgh, to fight at Catraeth around the year 600, and says he was one of the few who came back.",
    "In Welsh tradition he became one of the company of Arthur’s court. In the romance of Owain, it is Cynon who tells the story against himself: how he rode to the end of the world, found the fountain under the tree, poured water on the slab, and was overthrown by the black knight. His honest tale of failure sent his companion Owain son of Urien out to win the fountain and its lady.",
    "The Triads remember him as one of the Three Lovers of the Island of Britain, for his love of Morfudd daughter of Urien, Owain’s sister."],
   ["Rhyfelwr o’r Hen Ogledd oedd Cynon fab Clydno Eidyn, o deyrnasoedd y Brythoniaid yn yr hyn sydd heddiw’n dde’r Alban a gogledd Lloegr. Mae cerdd fawr Aneirin, Y Gododdin, yn ei glodfori ymysg gosgordd Mynyddog Mwynfawr a farchogodd o Ddin Eidyn i ymladd yng Nghatraeth tua’r flwyddyn 600, ac yn dweud ei fod yn un o’r ychydig a ddaeth yn ôl.",
    "Yn nhraddodiad Cymru daeth yn un o gwmni llys Arthur. Yn rhamant Owain, Cynon sy’n adrodd y stori yn ei erbyn ei hun: sut y marchogodd i ben draw’r byd, cael y ffynnon dan y pren, bwrw dŵr ar y llech, a chael ei fwrw i’r llawr gan y marchog du. Anfonodd ei hanes gonest am ei fethiant ei gydymaith Owain fab Urien allan i ennill y ffynnon a’i harglwyddes.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei gofio fel un o Dri Serchog Ynys Prydain, am ei gariad at Forfudd ferch Urien, chwaer Owain."]),
 P("ceridwen", "saints", ART, "Ceridwen", "Ceridwen", "Keeper of the cauldron of inspiration", "Ceidwad pair yr awen", "Ceridwen", "words",
   "Wife of Tegid Foel of Penllyn, who boiled the cauldron of inspiration for her son for a year and a day, chased Gwion Bach through many shapes, and gave birth to him again as Taliesin.",
   "Gwraig Tegid Foel o Benllyn, a ferwodd bair yr awen i’w mab am flwyddyn a diwrnod, ymlid Gwion Bach drwy lawer ffurf, a’i eni eto’n Daliesin.",
   ["Ceridwen lived with her husband Tegid Foel in Penllyn, in a home in the middle of Llyn Tegid, Bala Lake. They had a beautiful daughter, Creirwy, and a son, Morfran, so ugly that he was called Afagddu. Ceridwen was wise in the arts of magic, and to give her son a gift greater than beauty, she boiled a cauldron of inspiration and knowledge for a year and a day.",
    "Gwion Bach of Llanfair Caereinion stirred the cauldron, and at the end of the year the three precious drops flew out onto his finger and he tasted them. Ceridwen chased him in fury: as he became a hare, she became a greyhound; a fish, she an otter; a bird, she a hawk; and when he hid as a grain of wheat, she became a black hen and swallowed him. Nine months later she bore him again, and set him adrift on the sea in a leather bag. He was found in Gwyddno’s weir and named Taliesin.",
    "To the poets of medieval Wales, the cauldron of Ceridwen was the source of the awen, poetic inspiration itself. Taliesin, Cynddelw and other poets speak of it, and to this day the awen of Ceridwen is a symbol of Welsh poetry."],
   ["Trigai Ceridwen gyda’i gŵr Tegid Foel ym Mhenllyn, mewn cartref yng nghanol Llyn Tegid. Roedd ganddynt ferch hardd, Creirwy, a mab, Morfran, mor hyll fel y’i gelwid yn Afagddu. Roedd Ceridwen yn ddoeth yng nghelfyddyd hud, ac i roi i’w mab rodd fwy na harddwch, berwodd bair o awen a gwybodaeth am flwyddyn a diwrnod.",
    "Gwion Bach o Lanfair Caereinion a droai’r pair, ac ar ddiwedd y flwyddyn neidiodd y tri dafn gwerthfawr ar ei fys a’u profodd. Ymlidiodd Ceridwen ef yn ei llid: pan ddaeth ef yn ysgyfarnog, daeth hi’n filgi; ef yn bysgodyn, hi’n ddyfrgi; ef yn aderyn, hi’n hebog; a phan guddiodd yn ronyn o wenith, trodd hi’n iâr ddu a’i lyncu. Naw mis wedyn ganed ef ganddi eto, a’i roi ar y môr mewn cod o groen. Cafwyd ef yng nghored Gwyddno a’i enwi’n Daliesin.",
    "I feirdd Cymru’r Oesoedd Canol, pair Ceridwen oedd ffynhonnell yr awen ei hun. Mae Taliesin, Cynddelw a beirdd eraill yn sôn amdano, a hyd heddiw mae awen Ceridwen yn symbol o farddoniaeth Gymraeg."]),
 P("elis-gruffydd", "tudor", "c. 1490–after 1552", "Elis Gruffydd", "Elis Gruffydd", "The soldier of Calais who wrote down the Tale of Taliesin", "Y milwr o Calais a gofnododd Hanes Taliesin", "Elis_Gruffydd", "words",
   "A Welsh soldier and chronicler from Gronant in Flintshire who served in the garrison of Calais and wrote a vast Welsh chronicle of the world, in which he kept the Tale of Taliesin.",
   "Milwr a chroniclwr o Gymro o Ronant yn Sir y Fflint a wasanaethodd yng ngarsiwn Calais ac a ysgrifennodd gronicl Cymraeg anferth o’r byd, lle cadwodd Hanes Taliesin.",
   ["Elis Gruffydd was born about 1490 in the parish of Llanasa in Flintshire. As a young man he went to London and then to Calais, which was then held by the English crown, and served there for many years as a soldier of the garrison. He took part in the campaigns in France in the 1520s and 1540s and wrote about them as an eyewitness.",
    "In Calais he wrote, in Welsh, a huge chronicle of the history of the world from the Creation to his own day, in two great volumes now in the National Library of Wales. Into it he put many Welsh tales and traditions, and so he saved stories that would otherwise have been lost.",
    "The most famous of these is the Tale of Taliesin, the story of Ceridwen, Gwion Bach and the cauldron of inspiration, written down around 1548. Lady Charlotte Guest later added it to her Mabinogion, and through her it became the twelfth tale. Elis Gruffydd is honoured as one of the great Welsh writers of prose of the sixteenth century, and a proud Welshman far from home."],
   ["Ganed Elis Gruffydd tua 1490 ym mhlwyf Llanasa yn Sir y Fflint. Yn ŵr ifanc aeth i Lundain ac yna i Calais, a oedd bryd hynny yn nwylo coron Lloegr, a gwasanaethu yno am flynyddoedd lawer yn filwr yn y garsiwn. Cymerodd ran yn yr ymgyrchoedd yn Ffrainc yn y 1520au a’r 1540au ac ysgrifennu amdanynt fel llygad-dyst.",
    "Yn Calais ysgrifennodd, yn Gymraeg, gronicl anferth o hanes y byd o’r Creu hyd ei ddydd ei hun, mewn dwy gyfrol fawr sydd heddiw yn Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru. Rhoddodd ynddo lawer o chwedlau a thraddodiadau Cymreig, a thrwy hynny achub storïau a gollasid fel arall.",
    "Yr enwocaf o’r rhain yw Hanes Taliesin, stori Ceridwen, Gwion Bach a phair yr awen, a gofnodwyd tua 1548. Ychwanegodd yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest hi wedyn at ei Mabinogion, a thrwyddi hi daeth yn ddeuddegfed chwedl. Anrhydeddir Elis Gruffydd fel un o ryddieithwyr mawr Cymru yn yr unfed ganrif ar bymtheg, a Chymro balch ymhell o gartref."]),
]

